  "aT_attribution": {
    "verdict": "STRUCTURALLY_EXACT_NOT_A_VARIANCE_FRACTION",
    "explanation": "build_atac_graph_v2.py builds G_t = (L .* aT[None,:] @ H^T)^T with the diagonal zeroed. L (gene x peak link matrix) and H (peak x TF motif-hit matrix) are IDENTICAL across cell types; aT = log1p(mean accessibility over the sampled cells of type t) is the ONLY cell-type-dependent input. G_t is therefore an exactly linear function of aT: 100% of the between-cell-type variation in G is attributable to aT by construction. Asking 'what fraction of the proxy's variance is attributable to aT' is not cleanly decomposable into a percentage, because aT also carries the shared (type-invariant) accessibility level -- the same vector supplies both the shared and the type-specific parts. We therefore report the decomposable quantity instead: the fraction of total variance in the edge-by-cell-type matrix that is type-INVARIANT (between-edge) versus type-SPECIFIC (within-edge). See variance_decomposition_* per tissue. A single scalar 'aT explains X% of proxy variance' would be invented.",
    "aT_source_line": "src/v2/build_atac_graph_v2.py:153 (aT = np.log1p(X[cid].mean(0)))",
    "type_invariant_inputs": [
      "L (peak-gene link, PROM=2000bp + gene body)",
      "H (peak-motif hits, JASPAR2024, MOODS p<1e-5)"
    ]
  },
